			"text": "CAT9 — Groups can kill or save the model\nRQ3 · finding",
			"x": 140,
			"y": -80,
			"width": 400,
			"height": 100,
			"color": "4"
		},
		{
			"id": "cases",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "Cases\nS1 S4 S5 S6 S7 S8 S10",
			"x": -220,
			"y": -80,
			"width": 300,
			"height": 70,
			"color": "3"
		},
		{
			"id": "memo_group",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**Memos** (9) — Ctrl+hover a link to preview\n\n- [[S1M3]] — group conditions displaced modelling effort\n- [[S4M1]] — group work can kill modelling when code wins\n- [[S5M1]] — team coordination carries the modelling work\n- [[S5M4]] — AI and live tools in the group modelling loop\n- [[S6M4]] — pair structure and notation-specific sharing comfort\n- [[S7M3]] — graded then told: rushed teammate forced a full redo\n- [[S7M4]] — initiative-gated feedback and split-by-notation peer review\n- [[S8M2]] — ungraded practice makes checking optional\n- [[S10M5]] — exclusive notation ownership split the system",
			"x": -420,
			"y": 180,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 332,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "code_group",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "**Codes** (28) — Ctrl+hover a link to preview\n\n- [[S1-C09]] — sprint structures felt arbitrary in large group\n- [[S1-C10]] — stopped modelling and just finished the work\n- [[S1-C11]] — smaller team and shared model would help\n- [[S4-C02]] — group devalued model and abandoned it for code\n- [[S4-C09]] — early group sharing turned model into argument\n- [[S4-C11]] — modelling works when one person owns the design\n- [[S5-C03]] — group reads brief aloud then assigns drafter\n- [[S5-C06]] — good enough means teammates understand scope\n- [[S5-C09]] — shared Miro model with weekly diagram stand-ups\n- [[S5-C10]] — AI suggests options then team rewrites manually\n- [[S5-C11]] — live Miro editing lowered group modelling barrier\n- [[S5-C12]] — passive teammates block motivation regardless of domain\n- [[S5-C13]] — external readers push stakeholder vocabulary\n- [[S6-C06]] — documents alternative iStar decompositions with trade-offs\n- [[S6-C07]] — sharing comfort depends on notation familiarity\n- [[S6-C10]] — pair splits diagrams by type with weekly syncs\n- [[S7-C09]] — grade given before feedback so no resubmit\n- [[S7-C10]] — rushed teammate forced a from-scratch redo\n- [[S7-C11]] — more willing to share models than code\n- [[S7-C12]] — feedback does not fall from the sky\n- [[S7-C13]] — split-by-notation then peer review for defence\n- [[S8-C02]] — ungraded exercises rarely checked unless you ask\n- [[S8-C05]] — skipped semantic tools and used draw.io\n- [[S8-C06]] — walk-around feedback almost always meant changes\n- [[S8-C07]] — shares with friends not course-group strangers\n- [[S10-C01]] — thesis diagrams for communication not strict UML\n- [[S10-C12]] — split-by-notation made two versions of the system\n- [[S10-C13]] — explainability became a quality criterion",
			"x": 540,
			"y": 180,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 864,
			"color": "5"
		},
		{
			"id": "explainer",
			"type": "text",
			"text": "What this means\n\nIn groups, the diagram dies when teammates push to “just code,” stay passive, or freeze a rushed draft — and survives when someone owns it, the team reviews it, or they refuse exclusive ownership of one notation family. S9 is not used here.",
			"x": 80,
			"y": -360,
			"width": 520,
			"height": 180,
			"color": "1"
		}
	],
